WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.464
For the local coding demonstration, we begin with a new isolated folder.

00:00:04.464 --> 00:00:11.160
The task tells Codex exactly which public behavior references it may read and which
directory it may edit.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:16.740
It prohibits private folders, credentials, cloud configuration, browser data, global
installation, provider calls and publication.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:20.460
The build uses the installed local toolchain and synthetic data.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:24.552
This is a real coding invocation, not a decorative terminal animation.

00:00:24.552 --> 00:00:29.016
The resulting app, tests and run instructions are files we can inspect.

00:00:29.016 --> 00:00:32.736
Isolation also preserves the main course checkout and unrelated work.

00:00:32.736 --> 00:00:42.408
A coding agent's access should fit the task; a broad account login or powerful shell
does not automatically authorize it to inspect everything on the machine.

00:00:42.708 --> 00:00:53.472
The task packet describes a small foreground HTTP server bound to loopback, a
responsive incident inbox, the three seed records, search, priority filtering,
selection and a validated New ticket form.

00:00:53.472 --> 00:00:56.342
It asks for an honest disconnected AI state.

00:00:56.342 --> 00:01:04.235
It also asks for a small server-side harness demonstration with strict input, an
early change stop, a body-size limit and exact-origin handling.

00:01:04.235 --> 00:01:08.900
The normal path must return model not configured rather than a fake success.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:12.129
The browser cannot supply a model endpoint or credential.

00:01:12.129 --> 00:01:16.075
This packet makes the requested output and the stopping point clear.

00:01:16.075 --> 00:01:22.533
It does not ask the coding agent to provision a cloud service or silently acquire a
model key.

00:01:22.833 --> 00:01:27.070
This is the actual successful Codex invocation, with private paths replaced.

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:33.233
The first attempt failed before the build because the outer sandbox could not start
the client.

00:01:33.233 --> 00:01:36.699
The approved retry kept Codex’s inner workspace write sandbox.

00:01:36.699 --> 00:01:49.409
It ran version zero point one six six point zero with coding model G P T six point
one sol, read the reviewed task from standard input, and saved its final result
privately.

00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:51.720
The operator observed exit code zero.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:55.187
Codex produced the app, static files, tests and README.

00:01:55.187 --> 00:01:59.809
Its final report is an agent self report, not acceptance by itself.

00:01:59.809 --> 00:02:04.046
The next test and HTTP receipts independently check what was built.

00:02:04.046 --> 00:02:06.742
No runtime model call or deployment occurred.

00:02:07.042 --> 00:02:11.449
Here is the independent test output, not just the agent’s final message.

00:02:11.449 --> 00:02:17.326
The actual run reported twelve tests in zero point zero eight three seconds and O K.

00:02:17.326 --> 00:02:22.836
The selected cases check policy before model construction, unknown fields, Origin
rejection and body limits.

00:02:22.836 --> 00:02:28.713
A factory that would fail if invoked helps verify the blocked path never builds a
model.

00:02:28.713 --> 00:02:30.917
This is specific local code evidence.

00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:35.692
It does not prove a real language model ran or a trace arrived.

00:02:35.692 --> 00:02:39.365
Keep it separate from interactive browser checks and hosted acceptance.

00:02:39.365 --> 00:02:45.242
Next, actual HTTP responses check the server boundary, still without requesting a
provider or telemetry export.

00:02:45.542 --> 00:02:51.277
Use the generated README command to start the server in the foreground on an
available loopback port.

00:02:51.277 --> 00:02:55.326
A foreground process has a clear owner and a clear stop action.

00:02:55.326 --> 00:02:59.375
Open the actual local page and repeat the same synthetic ticket journey.

00:02:59.375 --> 00:03:03.761
Create the checkout check, search its title, filter and clear, then select payments.

00:03:03.761 --> 00:03:05.785
Inspect the visible disconnected AI state.

00:03:05.785 --> 00:03:12.533
The app is local, not publicly hosted, and its ticket state is in memory unless the
source explicitly implements otherwise.

00:03:12.533 --> 00:03:19.618
Do not open a public tunnel, add wildcard CORS, or bind to every network interface
simply to make a demo convenient.

00:03:19.618 --> 00:03:22.992
Those would change the access boundary and need separate review.

00:03:23.292 --> 00:03:44.167
Your turn: follow the manual task. Pause for more time.

00:03:44.167 --> 00:03:47.920
Nine loopback HTTP cases passed in the independent runner.

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:55.426
The page was available; the normal request was honestly disconnected and the restart
was blocked before model construction.

00:03:55.426 --> 00:03:57.928
Malformed and unexpected inputs were rejected.

00:03:57.928 --> 00:04:03.349
Foreign or missing Origin returned forbidden; an oversized body was rejected as
well.

00:04:03.349 --> 00:04:07.518
The table contains actual status codes from the saved receipt.

00:04:07.518 --> 00:04:12.522
The source was unchanged and the owned runner process was cleaned up.

00:04:12.522 --> 00:04:15.858
No provider call or telemetry export was requested.

00:04:15.858 --> 00:04:21.696
This is real HTTP evidence, not an AI answer and not a simulated terminal.

00:04:21.696 --> 00:04:26.283
Reproduce the request from your README and record your own output.

00:04:26.583 --> 00:04:32.418
The coding model that creates this local app does not automatically become the app's
runtime model.

00:04:32.418 --> 00:04:42.630
To investigate with a real provider, the server needs the learner's supported
endpoint or native runtime binding, the exact available model alias, authentication
and a reviewed harness adapter.

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:44.818
Keep values in private server configuration.

00:04:44.818 --> 00:04:50.288
Discover model capabilities rather than assuming that a model supports tool calls or
structured output.

00:04:50.288 --> 00:04:54.664
Use a small synthetic canary, bounded calls and no automatic inference retry.

00:04:54.664 --> 00:04:59.041
The current disconnected local teaching server intentionally stops short of that
gate.

00:04:59.041 --> 00:05:05.605
The course also contains a more complete local runtime reference; inspect its
configuration and tests before choosing it.

00:05:05.605 --> 00:05:09.617
Never silently replace a missing real provider with a scripted answer.

00:05:09.917 --> 00:05:15.788
The CLI can write a structured local event record for a run and show why it stopped.

00:05:15.788 --> 00:05:19.587
Local events are useful, but they are not remote telemetry readback.

00:05:19.587 --> 00:05:28.912
When a real exporter is configured, keep event names and attributes allowlisted and
do not dump prompts, answers, account details or key material into a terminal
recording.

00:05:28.912 --> 00:05:37.201
A trace identifier may be useful for private correlation, but the presentation can
show a reviewed crop instead of exposing the private project URL.

00:05:37.201 --> 00:05:42.727
Keep a safe receipt with the command, source revision, input class, observed output
and acceptance status.

00:05:42.727 --> 00:05:50.325
Do not commit raw logs or browser profiles just because the repository is private
today; the course is designed for public distribution.

00:05:50.625 --> 00:05:54.968
After a change, run the focused tests and the browser journey again.

00:05:54.968 --> 00:05:59.673
Verify the form, search, filter and selection before the AI or policy action.

00:05:59.673 --> 00:06:06.912
Check that the app stays responsive, failure states remain honest, and previously
added usage or trace controls have not disappeared.

00:06:06.912 --> 00:06:11.617
Keep the same project rather than rebuilding a disconnected example for each lesson.

00:06:11.617 --> 00:06:14.874
The user-facing result and server-side controls form one workflow.

00:06:14.874 --> 00:06:22.475
A useful handoff records how to launch it, which checks passed, which providers were
actually contacted and which gates remain unavailable.

00:06:22.475 --> 00:06:30.075
That is enough for another learner or agent to continue safely without treating an
optimistic status message as an execution receipt.

00:06:30.375 --> 00:06:33.441
Pause and open the command block beneath this chapter.

00:06:33.441 --> 00:06:34.803
Read it before executing.

00:06:34.803 --> 00:06:39.572
Use your own isolated folder, the installed supported toolchain and a free local
port.

00:06:39.572 --> 00:06:45.704
Run the task, inspect the files, execute the tests, launch the server and make the
synthetic API requests.

00:06:45.704 --> 00:06:48.088
Stop the foreground server when you finish.

00:06:48.088 --> 00:06:52.176
Do not paste secrets into the terminal recording or a public issue.

00:06:52.176 --> 00:06:58.307
If you lack a runtime model, keep the honest disconnected state and complete the
policy and frontend checks.

00:06:58.307 --> 00:07:00.010
Those are still useful capabilities.

00:07:00.010 --> 00:07:08.867
When you are ready to add the real backend, use the same app and a reviewed
configuration rather than starting again with a different, unexplained
implementation.

00:07:09.167 --> 00:07:30.000
Your turn: follow the manual task. Pause for more time.
